## Description

The App.tsx component has an infinite re-render loop that floods the console with:
```
[App] Tab restore useEffect triggered
```

This is caused by including the full `selectedTask` object in a useEffect dependency array while also calling `setSelectedTask()` inside the effect.

## Root Cause

In `apps/frontend/src/renderer/App.tsx` around line 389-399:

```tsx
useEffect(() => {
  if (selectedTask) {
    const updatedTask = tasks.find(
      (t) => t.id === selectedTask.id || t.specId === selectedTask.specId
    );
    if (updatedTask) {
      setSelectedTask(updatedTask);  // Creates new reference
    }
  }
}, [tasks, selectedTask?.id, selectedTask?.specId, selectedTask]); // BUG: selectedTask object in deps
```

The issue is:
1. `selectedTask` (the full object) is in the dependency array
2. `setSelectedTask(updatedTask)` creates a new object reference
3. New reference triggers the effect again → infinite loop

## Suggested Fix

Remove `selectedTask` from the dependency array and add a reference equality check:

```tsx
useEffect(() => {
  if (selectedTask) {
    const updatedTask = tasks.find(
      (t) => t.id === selectedTask.id || t.specId === selectedTask.specId
    );
    if (updatedTask && updatedTask !== selectedTask) {  // Add reference check
      setSelectedTask(updatedTask);
    }
  }
}, [tasks, selectedTask?.id, selectedTask?.specId]);  // Remove selectedTask object
```

The `selectedTask?.id` and `selectedTask?.specId` in the deps are sufficient to detect when we need to look for an updated task.

