## Accessibility Issue: Focus Management on View/Route Changes

**WCAG Level:** A  
**Severity:** Low  
**Category:** Dynamic Content (Category 8)

### Issue Description
When users switch between views (Kanban, Terminals, Roadmap, etc.) using keyboard shortcuts or navigation, focus management is not explicitly handled. Screen reader users may lose context about where they are in the application after a view change.

### User Impact
- **Affected Users:** Screen reader users, keyboard-only users
- **Severity:** After view changes, users may need to re-orient themselves to find the main content

### Violations Found

#### File: `apps/frontend/src/renderer/App.tsx`
**Lines:** Multiple - view change handlers

```tsx
<!-- Current observation -->
// When activeView changes, no focus management occurs
// Users must manually navigate to find the new content
```

**Issue:** View transitions don't programmatically move focus to the new content area.

---

### Recommended Fix

```tsx
<!-- Recommended approach -->
import { useRef, useEffect } from 'react';

// In App component
const mainContentRef = useRef<HTMLElement>(null);

// Effect to manage focus on view change
useEffect(() => {
  // Move focus to main content when view changes
  // Use a slight delay to ensure content is rendered
  const timeoutId = setTimeout(() => {
    if (mainContentRef.current) {
      mainContentRef.current.focus();
    }
  }, 100);

  return () => clearTimeout(timeoutId);
}, [activeView]);

// In JSX
<main 
  ref={mainContentRef}
  id="main-content"
  tabIndex={-1}
  className="flex-1 overflow-hidden outline-none"
  aria-label={t(`navigation:mainRegion.${activeView}`)}
>
```

**Changes Made:**
1. Add ref to main element for programmatic focus
2. Add `tabIndex={-1}` to allow focus without adding to tab order
3. Add useEffect to move focus on view change
4. Add `outline-none` to prevent focus ring on main (since it's not interactive)

### Alternative: Announce View Change
Instead of moving focus, announce the view change to screen readers:

```tsx
// Add an aria-live region for announcements
<div 
  aria-live="polite" 
  aria-atomic="true" 
  className="sr-only"
>
  {t(`navigation:viewChanged.${activeView}`)}
</div>
```

### Testing Instructions
1. Navigate to Kanban view
2. Enable screen reader
3. Use keyboard shortcut (e.g., "A" for Terminals)
4. Verify screen reader announces the new view
5. Verify focus is in a logical location
6. Test with various view combinations

### Resources
- [WCAG 2.4.3 Focus Order](https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG21/Understanding/focus-order.html)
- [ARIA Live Regions](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/Accessibility/ARIA/ARIA_Live_Regions)
- [Managing Focus for Accessibility](https://web.dev/articles/focus)

### Acceptance Criteria
- [ ] Focus moves to main content on view change OR view change is announced
- [ ] Screen reader users know which view they're in after navigation
- [ ] Keyboard navigation remains logical after view change
- [ ] No loss of context during transitions

